            <ArticleTitle>The Hip in Ice Hockey: A Current Concepts Review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1928-38</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arthro.2016.04.029</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0749-8063(16)30237-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ice hockey is a fast, physical sport with unique associated biomechanical demands often placing the hip in forced and repetitive supraphysiological ranges of motion. Ice hockey players commonly endure and are sidelined by nebulous groin injury or hip pain. Underlying causes can be chronic or acute and extra-articular, intra-articular, or &quot;hip-mimicking.&quot; This article serves to review common hip-related injuries in ice hockey. For each, we define the particular condition; comment on risk factors and preventive strategies; discuss key historical, physical examination, and imaging findings; and finally, suggest nonoperative and/or operative treatment plans.</AbstractText>
